<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.12.4: Possible Equation for a Function on a Graph</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01468</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.12.4: Possible Equation for a Function on a Graph</md:title>
    <md:uuid>8f167aef-188a-479a-81ee-d9721a38e931</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<para>Examine the three graphs representing three exponential functions: \(f\), \(g\), and \(h\).</para>
<para><figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c91699a5862502edaffc0fc9cafd5d89472eebad"/></media></figure></para>
<para>The functions \(f\) and \(h\) are defined as:</para>
<list>
  <item>\(f(x)=10 \cdot 2^x\)</item>
  <item>\(h(x)=20 \cdot 4^x\)</item>
</list>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="b6a65553-df6d-4524-a666-6848eb15aee5" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <exercise data-content-id="8349eeef-9ab1-4105-bcd7-065b0c411f0f" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-solution="\(g(x)=20 \cdot (2.5)^x\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(g(x)=20 \\cdot (1.5)^x\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(g(x)=20 \\cdot (2.5)^x\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(g(x)=10 \\cdot (3.5)^x\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(g(x)=20 \\cdot (4.5)^x\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which of the following could define function \(g\)? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! </para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below this line-->
  <solution> </solution>
  </div>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Explain your reasoning.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>The graph of \(g\) has the same \(y\)-intercept as the graph of \(h\), which is 20. It grows more quickly than \(f\) but more slowly than \(h\),<emphasis effect="italics"> </emphasis>so the growth factor must be greater than 2 but less than 4.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
